      {
        "id": "p39-q10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $\\dfrac{3x+1}{x}=\\dfrac{1}{x}+3$ for $x\\ne0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\dfrac{3x+1}{x}=3+\\dfrac1x=\\dfrac1x+3$, for $x\\ne0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{3x+1}{x}&=\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac1x\\\\\\frac{3x+1}{x}&=3+\\frac1x\\\\\\frac{3x+1}{x}&=\\frac1x+3\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      },
      {
        "id": "p39-q10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence sketch $y=\\dfrac{3x+1}{x}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=\\dfrac1x+3$: asymptotes $x=0$ and $y=3$; $x$-intercept $\\left(-\\dfrac13,0\\right)$; no $y$-intercept.",
          "worked": "Using part a, $y=\\dfrac1x+3$. Replace $y$ with $y-3$ in $y=\\dfrac1x$: $y-3=\\dfrac1x$. Translate the reciprocal graph 3 units up. The asymptotes are $x=0$ and $y=3$.\n\nFor the $x$-intercept:\n$$\\begin{align*}0&=\\frac1x+3\\\\\\frac1x&=-3\\\\x&=-\\frac13\\end{align*}$$\nThere is no $y$-intercept because $x=0$ is excluded. The positive-$x$ branch lies above $y=3$ and the negative-$x$ branch lies below it.",
          "solutionDiagrams": [
            {
